WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.960
&gt;&gt; So, our project is

00:00:06.961 --> 00:00:09.585
actually to develop
a domain-specific language

00:00:09.586 --> 00:00:13.185
for the class of applications

00:00:13.186 --> 00:00:15.605
comprising ML and data processing

00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:18.610
in constrained IoT devices.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.570
So, just to give
a background, actually,

00:00:22.571 --> 00:00:26.150
I had started
an industrial IoT startup,

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:29.775
and this is actually
my monitoring device.

00:00:29.776 --> 00:00:34.010
So, it has Wi-Fi and does
on-board data sensing,

00:00:34.011 --> 00:00:35.250
the communication
data processing,

00:00:35.251 --> 00:00:37.215
everything is done on the device.

00:00:37.216 --> 00:00:38.980
So, it actually had a

00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:41.300
programmable rule engine
data aggregation.

00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:43.880
So, this is actually
my starting point

00:00:43.881 --> 00:00:46.100
for me to work into
the language aspect

00:00:46.101 --> 00:00:51.755
of IoT data processing
ML applications.

00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:54.160
So, the problem and
the motivation.

00:00:54.161 --> 00:00:56.930
So, it's computing applications
are some more and

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:59.750
more becoming
increasingly popular

00:00:59.751 --> 00:01:01.165
in constrained IoT devices.

00:01:01.166 --> 00:01:03.380
A prime example is
Interactive Cane application

00:01:03.381 --> 00:01:05.670
developed by Microsoft
research right here.

00:01:05.671 --> 00:01:09.380
Actually, you can see
the device developed by Mecer.

00:01:09.381 --> 00:01:13.805
So, basically it allows
visually challenged people to

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.935
interact with their phone

00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:19.240
like gesture with
their walking stick,

00:01:19.241 --> 00:01:22.390
and ML is actually
implemented on the device,

00:01:22.391 --> 00:01:25.770
which only has
a few kilobytes of RAM.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.100
So, the constrained
embedded applications

00:01:31.101 --> 00:01:32.230
have limited resources.

00:01:32.231 --> 00:01:34.275
There is no resource
virtualization.

00:01:34.276 --> 00:01:36.685
Energy, timing are
important considerations,

00:01:36.686 --> 00:01:38.935
and our motivation is the lack of

00:01:38.936 --> 00:01:41.810
current programming tools
to support development of

00:01:41.811 --> 00:01:44.180
modular data pipelines with

00:01:44.181 --> 00:01:45.500
compile-time feedback to

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.770
the programmer on
resource usages.

00:01:47.771 --> 00:01:50.630
As Alan Perlis famously said,

00:01:50.631 --> 00:01:51.800
"Programmers know the value of

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:52.920
everything and
the cost of nothing."

00:01:52.921 --> 00:01:57.050
So, we want to make the program
aware of the cost of

00:01:57.051 --> 00:02:00.110
memory and compute
an energy overheads

00:02:00.111 --> 00:02:03.030
of enrolling their application.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.880
So, there are three
overarching objectives.

00:02:06.881 --> 00:02:10.700
One is to provide
compile-time feedback

00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:12.745
on power timing and
resource usage.

00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:15.065
So, obviously, through
static analysis.

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:17.590
Second, to enable
optimizations not support or

00:02:17.591 --> 00:02:20.510
unlikely to be done by
standard compilers.

00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:24.275
Third, which is important
for a programmer,

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:26.570
clarity, concision and
programmer convenience.

00:02:26.571 --> 00:02:28.550
Actually, as of
now we have ported

00:02:28.551 --> 00:02:30.430
the entire Interactive
Cane application

00:02:30.431 --> 00:02:31.855
written by Microsoft Research.

00:02:31.856 --> 00:02:33.895
Original applications
are written in C++.

00:02:33.896 --> 00:02:35.230
So, we have

00:02:35.231 --> 00:02:37.790
a fully working ported
application in our DSL,

00:02:37.791 --> 00:02:39.780
which we will demo
at the end, and we

00:02:39.781 --> 00:02:43.035
have one fourth code
size reduction.

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:45.690
So, we just kind of
important for programmer,

00:02:45.691 --> 00:02:47.790
program maintenance and
debugging and so on.

00:02:47.791 --> 00:02:49.690
Also work in progress,

00:02:49.691 --> 00:02:52.000
short paper on the language
has been accepted in

00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:55.360
ACM SIGBED, EMSOFT conference.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.830
So, what is the challenge in
static analysis of power,

00:03:01.831 --> 00:03:03.690
timing, resource usage
in embedded systems?

00:03:03.691 --> 00:03:07.280
So, we need to able
to reason about I/O.

00:03:07.281 --> 00:03:10.220
Of course, we can
analyze a program and

00:03:10.221 --> 00:03:14.465
a compare the worst case
execution time of computations,

00:03:14.466 --> 00:03:17.390
but we also need to be
able to reason about I/O.

00:03:17.391 --> 00:03:19.190
It's impractical to integrate

00:03:19.191 --> 00:03:21.780
a full hardware and
model into the analysis.

00:03:21.781 --> 00:03:23.855
Also, there is a need to use

00:03:23.856 --> 00:03:27.045
low-level C/C++ libraries
for hardware access.

00:03:27.046 --> 00:03:29.330
So, interoperability
with existing libraries

00:03:29.331 --> 00:03:31.870
are very important for
the language to be adopted.

00:03:31.871 --> 00:03:33.710
So, we have come up
with this concept

00:03:33.711 --> 00:03:36.390
of effect specifications.

00:03:36.391 --> 00:03:38.120
So, the effects of library calls

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:39.260
can be made explicit through

00:03:39.261 --> 00:03:40.700
effect specifications and we

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:42.050
will show some code snippets.

00:03:42.051 --> 00:03:44.030
So, we can actually

00:03:44.031 --> 00:03:45.080
annotate different kinds of

00:03:45.081 --> 00:03:46.819
effects like
resource acquisition,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.330
which is useful for analyzing
resource conflicts.

00:03:49.331 --> 00:03:51.470
Also, we'll give an
example of how it can also

00:03:51.471 --> 00:03:53.850
be used to analyze
security and privacy also.

00:03:53.851 --> 00:03:57.350
CPU time consumption,
power state change,

00:03:57.351 --> 00:03:58.950
read/write on memory objects,

00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:00.720
so this is useful for
memory optimization.

00:04:00.721 --> 00:04:02.690
So, we know that whether

00:04:02.691 --> 00:04:04.410
an object being passed
through library code

00:04:04.411 --> 00:04:06.500
is getting right door updated by

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:10.040
the library code. Timed events.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.040
So, this is an example of
our effect specification for

00:04:15.041 --> 00:04:17.170
a few Arduino library
function calls

00:04:17.171 --> 00:04:20.755
because eigen applications
actually uses Arduino library.

00:04:20.756 --> 00:04:23.410
So, this actually
the fastest statement

00:04:23.411 --> 00:04:24.980
assigns the effect.

00:04:24.981 --> 00:04:28.580
So, this means that if
we call pinPeripheral n,

00:04:28.581 --> 00:04:31.120
which is an Arduino
library function,

00:04:31.121 --> 00:04:34.685
it has the effect of acquiring
the pin without reason.

00:04:34.686 --> 00:04:36.040
So pin, this is actually

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:39.000
the resource capability class
which can be refined.

00:04:39.001 --> 00:04:42.115
So, we can for example, define
with specific operations.

00:04:42.116 --> 00:04:43.635
So, this means that
this particular

00:04:43.636 --> 00:04:45.310
the model which invokes
this pinPeripheral

00:04:45.311 --> 00:04:47.820
actually acquires pin capability

00:04:47.821 --> 00:04:49.590
with the resource address then.

00:04:49.591 --> 00:04:52.225
Similarly, serial print line.

00:04:52.226 --> 00:04:54.345
So, there are different kinds
of capabilities here.

00:04:54.346 --> 00:04:56.650
The other is CPU time.

00:04:56.651 --> 00:04:58.520
So, if we have poldio,

00:04:58.521 --> 00:05:01.300
program dio basically
that consume CPU cycles.

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:04.555
So that effect can actually
be specified like this,

00:05:04.556 --> 00:05:07.155
and this has a memory effect so

00:05:07.156 --> 00:05:11.460
getFIFOBytes actually updates
the buf memory effect,

00:05:11.461 --> 00:05:14.310
CPU cycle effect
power state change.

00:05:14.311 --> 00:05:15.970
So, these kind of side effects

00:05:15.971 --> 00:05:18.880
of library function
calls can actually

00:05:18.881 --> 00:05:24.730
be made explicit through
our fixed specification.

00:05:24.731 --> 00:05:29.100
So, that is one of our
contributions, key contributions.

00:05:29.101 --> 00:05:31.390
Second, is do optimizations

00:05:31.391 --> 00:05:33.725
that a traditional
compiler may not do.

00:05:33.726 --> 00:05:38.600
So, actually one of the
things we've done is,

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:41.195
we actually have
a compiler managed heap.

00:05:41.196 --> 00:05:44.500
So, if you want to reuse memory
in a traditional program,

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:46.190
you have to actually
do garbage collection

00:05:46.191 --> 00:05:48.495
or explicit memory management.

00:05:48.496 --> 00:05:51.185
But here the compiler actually

00:05:51.186 --> 00:05:53.465
compares the lifetimes
of objects like

00:05:53.466 --> 00:05:56.135
matrices and vectors and

00:05:56.136 --> 00:05:58.870
reuses them. Of course,
this is similar.

00:05:58.871 --> 00:06:01.360
As Shriram said, this is
similar to register allocation,

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:03.410
but difference is
that registered as

00:06:03.411 --> 00:06:05.345
an atomic object whereas

00:06:05.346 --> 00:06:07.580
an array is actually
not an atomic object.

00:06:07.581 --> 00:06:09.290
So, a part of the
array could be reused

00:06:09.291 --> 00:06:11.270
by some other object,

00:06:11.271 --> 00:06:12.680
and the rest of the array block

00:06:12.681 --> 00:06:15.245
could reused by
some other object and so on.

00:06:15.246 --> 00:06:18.380
An interesting issue we

00:06:18.381 --> 00:06:19.460
encountered here was that in

00:06:19.461 --> 00:06:20.540
traditional memory allocation,

00:06:20.541 --> 00:06:21.660
we have this fragmentation,

00:06:21.661 --> 00:06:23.770
internal fragmentation,
external fragmentation.

00:06:23.771 --> 00:06:27.115
Here, we also have encountered
lifetime fragmentation.

00:06:27.116 --> 00:06:30.050
So, for example,
if I try to reuse

00:06:30.051 --> 00:06:35.690
memory across two objects
having different lifetimes,

00:06:35.691 --> 00:06:37.380
and if they are far
apart, actually,

00:06:37.381 --> 00:06:39.680
we're actually losing some amount

00:06:39.681 --> 00:06:41.490
of time when the buffer
is actually free,

00:06:41.491 --> 00:06:42.675
not used by anybody.

00:06:42.676 --> 00:06:43.910
So, we actually want to reduce

00:06:43.911 --> 00:06:46.345
lifetime fragmentation as well
as internal fragmentation.

00:06:46.346 --> 00:06:49.475
Second is, we can have
operation of our allocation.

00:06:49.476 --> 00:06:53.410
For example, this is
a [inaudible] operation,

00:06:53.411 --> 00:06:55.190
concatenation of two vectors.

00:06:55.191 --> 00:06:56.720
So, if A concat B occurs,

00:06:56.721 --> 00:06:58.260
then A and B can

00:06:58.261 --> 00:07:00.050
actually be allocated
in adjacent memory,

00:07:00.051 --> 00:07:02.465
and the operation can
actually be eliminated.

00:07:02.466 --> 00:07:07.080
Then fusion of matrix-vector
loops, then in-place update.

00:07:07.081 --> 00:07:08.190
So, in-place for example,

00:07:08.191 --> 00:07:09.730
let us say we are casting

00:07:09.731 --> 00:07:13.095
an integer array to float array.

00:07:13.096 --> 00:07:14.825
So, the alignment sizes are same.

00:07:14.826 --> 00:07:17.630
We can actually do
an in-place update,

00:07:17.631 --> 00:07:21.375
so again, reducing
the memory usage.

00:07:21.376 --> 00:07:23.780
Then finally, generate
efficient state machine

00:07:23.781 --> 00:07:28.310
that automatically enters CPU
sleep mode when it is idle.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.970
So basically, a traditional
static electricity

00:07:31.971 --> 00:07:34.210
actually allocates along
the linear address line.

00:07:34.211 --> 00:07:37.010
We actually allocate,
our allocator works in

00:07:37.011 --> 00:07:41.910
the space where the y-axis is
actually the address line,

00:07:41.911 --> 00:07:45.020
and x-axis is actually
the pipeline lifetime.

00:07:45.021 --> 00:07:48.515
So, we actually want to
fill this entire area.

00:07:48.516 --> 00:07:50.130
So, basically we want to optimize

00:07:50.131 --> 00:07:51.320
by filling this entire area,

00:07:51.321 --> 00:07:56.175
and here you can
see that these two.

00:07:56.176 --> 00:07:57.525
So, this is our address,

00:07:57.526 --> 00:07:59.690
you can see these two
buffers actually share

00:07:59.691 --> 00:08:02.535
the same address region

00:08:02.536 --> 00:08:05.110
because they have
different lifetimes.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:08.350
There's actually
the memory layout output

00:08:08.351 --> 00:08:10.520
from our ID so it's
automatically generated.

00:08:10.521 --> 00:08:14.175
&gt;&gt; Question. Is lifetime
based on the method in which-

00:08:14.176 --> 00:08:18.585
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, basically we do
a whole program analysis.

00:08:18.586 --> 00:08:20.040
&gt;&gt; Is it later a form of

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:21.840
escape analysis
that you are doing?

00:08:21.841 --> 00:08:29.315
&gt;&gt; No. So, basically
the pipeline applications,

00:08:29.316 --> 00:08:32.525
we basically have
a cyclic execution,

00:08:32.526 --> 00:08:34.220
same pipeline repeatedly, right?

00:08:34.221 --> 00:08:36.815
So, if you take one
execution of the pipeline,

00:08:36.816 --> 00:08:40.335
lifetime is the duration

00:08:40.336 --> 00:08:42.970
in the program execution
meant that variable active,

00:08:42.971 --> 00:08:45.490
either defined or used.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.560
Similarly, we have a very

00:08:52.561 --> 00:08:55.245
basic worst case
execution time analysis.

00:08:55.246 --> 00:08:57.210
Software as a computer analysis,

00:08:57.211 --> 00:08:59.805
pretty simple because
CPU is pretty simple.

00:08:59.806 --> 00:09:02.240
There isn't caching
and those kind

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:06.585
of advanced features
are not there.

00:09:06.586 --> 00:09:08.685
But we have lot of I/O.

00:09:08.686 --> 00:09:10.270
So, basically we
incorporate I/O into

00:09:10.271 --> 00:09:13.615
our timing analysis by
using our annotations.

00:09:13.616 --> 00:09:16.635
So, for example, the acquisition
does a blocking I/O,

00:09:16.636 --> 00:09:18.370
and because of
our annotation we can

00:09:18.371 --> 00:09:20.140
actually capture that
into our analysis.

00:09:20.141 --> 00:09:21.815
So, we know that mpu_acq

00:09:21.816 --> 00:09:24.425
actually takes this
many CPU cycles.

00:09:24.426 --> 00:09:27.690
So, this is again the
output from our IDE.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.405
&gt;&gt; Can I ask you a question?

00:09:29.406 --> 00:09:29.810
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:09:29.811 --> 00:09:32.750
&gt;&gt; Do you do this kind of
analysis moderately or

00:09:32.751 --> 00:09:33.970
you just in line everything and

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:35.605
just do this as
one method finally?

00:09:35.606 --> 00:09:38.065
&gt;&gt; Yeah. We do whole program
analysis, non-modular.

00:09:38.066 --> 00:09:39.535
&gt;&gt; You in line
everything and you just

00:09:39.536 --> 00:09:40.735
then analyze as one method?

00:09:40.736 --> 00:09:45.665
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Actually, it's
done in a modular fashion.

00:09:45.666 --> 00:09:47.245
So, we don't really
in-line anything.

00:09:47.246 --> 00:09:49.440
&gt;&gt; So, for every method
you compute an effect?

00:09:49.441 --> 00:09:50.180
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:09:50.181 --> 00:09:51.130
&gt;&gt; Somebody has something like

00:09:51.131 --> 00:09:52.685
that maybe just chain them up?

00:09:52.686 --> 00:09:55.435
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly. So, it's
done in a modular fashion.

00:09:55.436 --> 00:09:58.590
But actually we follow
the chain of executions.

00:09:58.591 --> 00:10:00.310
So, we complete in
a modular fashion

00:10:00.311 --> 00:10:03.520
and then compute aggregate.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:06.580
The last, but not the least,

00:10:06.581 --> 00:10:08.700
is clarity in
programmer convenience.

00:10:08.701 --> 00:10:09.560
So, we have built-in

00:10:09.561 --> 00:10:11.635
matrix/vector operations
with dimensions.

00:10:11.636 --> 00:10:14.340
So, we have dimensions
integrated into the type system.

00:10:14.341 --> 00:10:15.990
So, if I'm trying to multiply

00:10:15.991 --> 00:10:18.010
two matrices which do not
have compatible dimensions,

00:10:18.011 --> 00:10:19.720
we can give a compile-time error.

00:10:19.721 --> 00:10:22.130
We actually have
a built-in circular vector.

00:10:22.131 --> 00:10:24.010
Circular vector are
usually used in

00:10:24.011 --> 00:10:26.685
data acquisition applications
because we want to keep,

00:10:26.686 --> 00:10:29.120
in a latest, an elements.

00:10:29.121 --> 00:10:32.120
So, and can we use like
a linear vector by

00:10:32.121 --> 00:10:35.410
the programmer and we have
modular pipeline stage.

00:10:35.411 --> 00:10:37.325
So, I'll show you
some code snippets

00:10:37.326 --> 00:10:39.650
with declarative
pipeline specification.

00:10:39.651 --> 00:10:42.150
Separation of errors handling
policy from the main code,

00:10:42.151 --> 00:10:43.780
and we can actually
have a library

00:10:43.781 --> 00:10:45.105
of pre-defined pipeline stages,

00:10:45.106 --> 00:10:46.585
we'll show you a few examples.

00:10:46.586 --> 00:10:48.550
Interoperability with
C/C++ libraries,

00:10:48.551 --> 00:10:51.010
and we actually have
built-type browser-based IDE.

00:10:51.011 --> 00:10:57.415
So, individual modules we

00:10:57.416 --> 00:10:59.650
are actually calling
flow modules.

00:10:59.651 --> 00:11:01.490
So, there is a flow routine,

00:11:01.491 --> 00:11:04.165
there's acquisition routines
doesn't take any input.

00:11:04.166 --> 00:11:07.710
So, once a stage is completed,

00:11:07.711 --> 00:11:10.115
data generation actually, can

00:11:10.116 --> 00:11:12.180
pass the data to the next stage
by invoking the next.

00:11:12.181 --> 00:11:14.740
So, this is like asynchronously
training this thing,

00:11:14.741 --> 00:11:16.620
and if it doesn't want
it doesn't need to call.

00:11:16.621 --> 00:11:18.440
So, the module can actually

00:11:18.441 --> 00:11:20.240
control whether the generacy
flow to the next stage

00:11:20.241 --> 00:11:22.360
or control like should

00:11:22.361 --> 00:11:24.805
start execution the beginning
of the pipeline.

00:11:24.806 --> 00:11:26.830
Then, there is a next stage.

00:11:26.831 --> 00:11:28.880
So, past data's actually
flows to the next stage,

00:11:28.881 --> 00:11:31.010
and processes, and so on.

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:35.500
Then, icane applications
actually can be

00:11:35.501 --> 00:11:37.570
declared as a pipeline
specification

00:11:37.571 --> 00:11:39.805
here in our language.

00:11:39.806 --> 00:11:42.315
You can see the different stages,

00:11:42.316 --> 00:11:44.795
and these are like
pre-built reusable modules.

00:11:44.796 --> 00:11:47.750
So only if data has
changed from the past,

00:11:47.751 --> 00:11:49.275
the next stage will be executed.

00:11:49.276 --> 00:11:50.855
We can also limit the rate.

00:11:50.856 --> 00:11:53.590
So we can actually do

00:11:53.591 --> 00:11:56.540
some very simple privacy
kind of analysis.

00:11:56.541 --> 00:11:58.420
For example, this our

00:11:58.421 --> 00:12:00.885
only module which
acquires sensor port.

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:03.065
We know that from
the effects specification.

00:12:03.066 --> 00:12:05.280
And this our only module
which acquires data port,

00:12:05.281 --> 00:12:07.305
that means this is
the only module that transmits.

00:12:07.306 --> 00:12:10.060
