specification,

00:26:21.261 --> 00:26:23.245
then include in the analysis.

00:26:23.246 --> 00:26:26.190
So, it needs to be done
once for a library,

00:26:26.191 --> 00:26:28.000
and then reused in
the application.

00:26:28.001 --> 00:26:30.425
That was a- yes.

00:26:30.426 --> 00:26:33.830
&gt;&gt; Traditionally Compilers
have shied away from doing

00:26:33.831 --> 00:26:35.750
inter-procedural whole
program analysis

00:26:35.751 --> 00:26:39.290
because that is closer
to the compilation type.

00:26:39.291 --> 00:26:42.850
So what is the
compilation time frame

00:26:42.851 --> 00:26:46.165
of a linear quadratic
method programs?

00:26:46.166 --> 00:26:48.365
&gt;&gt; I haven't done
an analysis on that.

00:26:48.366 --> 00:26:49.650
Of course, some of our algorithms

00:26:49.651 --> 00:26:53.055
are definitely
non-linear right now.

00:26:53.056 --> 00:26:55.770
But one reason we are
able to- actually,

00:26:55.771 --> 00:26:57.765
if you look at NesC, for example.

00:26:57.766 --> 00:26:59.885
NesC actually did
whole program analysis

00:26:59.886 --> 00:27:01.980
for many other- because recently,

00:27:01.981 --> 00:27:03.825
the program size is small.

00:27:03.826 --> 00:27:06.530
Because we are actually, unlike

00:27:06.531 --> 00:27:07.710
a traditional folk, for example,

00:27:07.711 --> 00:27:09.150
if we look at
a traditional application,

00:27:09.151 --> 00:27:12.300
it may run to 100,000 or
millions of lines of code.

00:27:12.301 --> 00:27:15.080
But, whereas here,
the application size is less,

00:27:15.081 --> 00:27:16.990
so that is one reason why we are

00:27:16.991 --> 00:27:19.435
actually able to afford
our whole program analysis.

00:27:19.436 --> 00:27:21.135
Another reason is that,

00:27:21.136 --> 00:27:22.580
unlike a traditional program,

00:27:22.581 --> 00:27:24.255
an embedded program is

00:27:24.256 --> 00:27:27.600
closed over all the
important parameters.

00:27:27.601 --> 00:27:30.750
For example, allocation size and

00:27:30.751 --> 00:27:32.285
all those things are
actually specified

00:27:32.286 --> 00:27:34.200
somewhere as a constant
in the program.

00:27:34.201 --> 00:27:35.050
Whereas, if we look at

00:27:35.051 --> 00:27:36.370
a traditional
application for example,

00:27:36.371 --> 00:27:39.080
the application we
allocate a link list based

00:27:39.081 --> 00:27:40.150
on some user input or

00:27:40.151 --> 00:27:41.960
the size may be based
on some user input,

00:27:41.961 --> 00:27:43.410
or it may read from some file.

00:27:43.411 --> 00:27:45.640
So, it's not really
possible to do

00:27:45.641 --> 00:27:48.015
this kind of analysis in
a traditional context at all.

00:27:48.016 --> 00:27:53.440
But here because
the program is closed,

00:27:53.441 --> 00:27:55.090
working in a closed environment,

00:27:55.091 --> 00:27:58.370
we are actually able to
do this kind of analysis.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:02.100
It is closed because of
two reasons, one is of course,

00:28:02.101 --> 00:28:03.475
is because of constraints,

00:28:03.476 --> 00:28:04.945
you don't have enough memory.

00:28:04.946 --> 00:28:06.280
Second is safety.

00:28:06.281 --> 00:28:08.550
Actually, if you have
a look at- so there is

00:28:08.551 --> 00:28:13.690
a C dialect called MISRA C by

00:28:13.691 --> 00:28:17.235
Motor Industries Centers
Association for safety purposes.

00:28:17.236 --> 00:28:18.490
That actually disallows the old

00:28:18.491 --> 00:28:20.185
dynamic aspects of C language,

00:28:20.186 --> 00:28:22.145
pointer manipulation,
memory allocation.

00:28:22.146 --> 00:28:23.540
So, that the other concern is,

00:28:23.541 --> 00:28:28.695
not more than
memory constraints safety.

00:28:28.696 --> 00:28:30.950
But anyway, coming
back to the point is,

00:28:30.951 --> 00:28:32.270
because the program
size is small,

00:28:32.271 --> 00:28:35.250
we are able to end because
the program is closed.

